From 991ad39f57d27606bc8bc744e5c35923f9646f39 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: LiBr <me@nvme0n1p.dev>
Date: Sun, 11 Oct 2026 18:14:16 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 08/57] Z 6III: the firmwares' own logs as they are written;
 past the GUI draws

Logs. Neither processor prints a boot log, but both keep binary ones:
the RTOS's application layer writes 32-byte records to per-module rings
in heap memory through one function, sub_40C06924(object, level, a,
code, b) -- 84 modules' wrappers end there, the camera controller's
among them -- the kernel has TOPPERS syslog (syslog_wri_log at
0x401842f8; fully enabled, never called on a healthy boot), and the body
MCU keeps an error ring through sub_80214C0(code, value). The rings sit
in runtime heap, scattered over 84 objects, so instead of mapping them
the new TCG plugin z63-tools/plugins/z63log.c hooks those three entry
points and writes one line per call: module (the object's first u16),
level, code, arguments and the calling address, in host-time order.
Build with z63-tools/plugins/build.sh; load with
-plugin z63-tools/plugins/libz63log.dylib,out=<file>, or Z63_LOG=<file>
with gui.sh. The build has to be configured with -Dplugins=true (this
tree's is now); without the plugin loaded nothing changes. A boot gives
some 9000 records in two minutes, the sensor-ready timeout 0x1EA7 among
them.

Graphics. The GUI's movie shooting screen uses two image states the
Z 9 never did:
- 0x48 reg 0x640 = 1 with eight words at 0x641..0x648 (float32 >> 13,
  from sub_40243FF0 through sub_402226B0). The first sets the GUI sends
  are four (1.0, 0.0) pairs -- read as a per-channel scale/offset, the
  identity -- and those are drawn as if 0x640 were 0; anything else
  still stops, and the state is reported in full. The packet itself is
  stored now instead of stopping at once.
- an RGBA source with the sampler words the model only knows for A8
  stencils (0x610 = 0x39, 0x611 = 2, 0x630 = 3). Not decoded.
For runs that need to get past such draws, -machine
gfx-skip-unmodelled=on drops a draw the model refuses at its commit --
no pixels written, fences complete, each reason named once -- instead
of stopping. Off by default.

Where live view stands: CSEQ powers the sensor up, FrontEnd starts the
0x150C front-end engine (PPC_0) and waits 400 ms for its first-frame
completion (resource events 0x422D0002/3 -> sub_410B05A8 -> FrontEnd's
callbacks -> 0xA04072F10 bit 4). That needs the sensor to be streaming,
which nothing models yet, so FrontEnd logs 7624 and CSEQ times out with
0x1EA7.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
